WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.498
Before You Connect an App: A 15-Minute Permission Inventory. “Connect

2
00:00:03.498 --> 00:00:06.995
your account” is one of the shortest sentences in modern

3
00:00:06.995 --> 00:00:10.493
software. It can also be one of the most consequential.

4
00:00:10.493 --> 00:00:13.990
A connected application may be able to read a calendar,

5
00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.488
write a document, see contacts, access files, send messages, use

6
00:00:17.488 --> 00:00:20.985
location, or retain information after the moment you needed the

7
00:00:20.985 --> 00:00:24.483
feature. The exact scope depends on the app, account, device,

8
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:27.981
settings, and permission you approve. The answer is not to

9
00:00:27.981 --> 00:00:31.478
treat every connection as dangerous. The answer is to treat

10
00:00:31.478 --> 00:00:34.976
a connection as a decision about access—not as a harmless

11
00:00:34.976 --> 00:00:38.473
decoration around a new tool. Take fifteen minutes before the

12
00:00:38.473 --> 00:00:41.971
next connection Minute What to check 0–3 Write the one

13
00:00:41.971 --> 00:00:45.469
task you want the app to help with. If the

14
00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:48.966
task is vague, do not grant broad access yet. 3–6

15
00:00:48.966 --> 00:00:52.464
Read the permission screen. Is the app asking to view,

16
00:00:52.464 --> 00:00:55.961
create, edit, send, delete, or continuously access information? 6–9 Ask

17
00:00:55.961 --> 00:00:59.459
whether a lower-access option, sample file, non-sensitive account, or manual

18
00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:02.956
export would accomplish the same task. 9–12 Record the connection:

19
00:01:02.956 --> 00:01:06.454
app name, account, permissions, person responsible, date granted, and how

20
00:01:06.454 --> 00:01:09.952
to revoke it. 12–15 Set a review date. Remove the

21
00:01:09.952 --> 00:01:13.449
connection if the tool is no longer useful, the scope

22
00:01:13.449 --> 00:01:16.947
changes, or you cannot explain what it can access. Ask

23
00:01:16.947 --> 00:01:20.444
what an app can see and what it can do

24
00:01:20.444 --> 00:01:23.942
Reading access and writing access are different. A tool that

25
00:01:23.942 --> 00:01:27.439
can see one document is different from a tool that

26
00:01:27.439 --> 00:01:30.937
can search a drive. A tool that can suggest a

27
00:01:30.937 --> 00:01:34.435
calendar entry is different from one that can create, edit,

28
00:01:34.435 --> 00:01:37.932
or delete events. The Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency warns

29
00:01:37.932 --> 00:01:41.430
that apps can access sensitive information and recommends a simple

30
00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.927
baseline: install only the apps you need, remove those you

31
00:01:44.927 --> 00:01:48.425
do not use, and deny access to data or functions

32
00:01:48.425 --> 00:01:51.923
you do not want an app to have. That is

33
00:01:51.923 --> 00:01:55.420
a practical rule because access tends to outlast the excitement

34
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.918
of a first test. It is also useful to look

35
00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:02.415
for the actual permission categories. Depending on the device or

36
00:02:02.415 --> 00:02:05.913
service, those may include contacts, files, camera, microphone, messages, calendar,

37
00:02:05.913 --> 00:02:09.410
location, health data, or the ability to create and delete

38
00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.908
records. A familiar brand name does not change the question:

39
00:02:12.908 --> 00:02:16.406
what does this connection permit? Keep the first test small

40
00:02:16.406 --> 00:02:19.903
For a new AI tool or connected workflow, begin with

41
00:02:19.903 --> 00:02:23.401
material that would not create a serious problem if it

42
00:02:23.401 --> 00:02:26.898
were mishandled: a fictional project, a public document, a blank

43
00:02:26.898 --> 00:02:30.396
calendar, or a copy of a non-sensitive file. Do not

44
00:02:30.396 --> 00:02:33.893
paste credentials, tokens, client records, member information, private call notes,

45
00:02:33.893 --> 00:02:37.391
payment information, or confidential documents into a test simply to

46
00:02:37.391 --> 00:02:40.889
see whether a workflow feels impressive. A small test can

47
00:02:40.889 --> 00:02:44.386
answer a real question without turning the test into a

48
00:02:44.386 --> 00:02:47.884
data-transfer decision. Use the controls you already have Many account

49
00:02:47.884 --> 00:02:51.381
providers offer a place to review linked apps. Google’s account

50
00:02:51.381 --> 00:02:54.879
guidance says people can review and update linked apps from

51
00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:58.377
their Google Account and see the information they share with

52
00:02:58.377 --> 00:03:01.874
applications from other developers. Other providers and device operating systems

53
00:03:01.874 --> 00:03:05.372
offer their own connection and permission controls. Those controls are

54
00:03:05.372 --> 00:03:08.869
not a guarantee that every app is safe or suitable.

55
00:03:08.869 --> 00:03:12.367
They are a way to make access visible, reduce unnecessary

56
00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:15.864
scope, and keep a revocation path available. The permission inventory

57
00:03:15.864 --> 00:03:19.362
Use this one-line record for each connection: App: ____ Account:

58
00:03:19.362 --> 00:03:22.860
____ Task: ____ Access granted: ____ Owner: ____ Review date:

59
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.357
____ Revocation path: ____ That record will not prevent every

60
00:03:26.357 --> 00:03:29.855
mistake. It does make a simple promise to yourself: you

61
00:03:29.855 --> 00:03:33.352
will know which door you opened, why you opened it,

62
00:03:33.352 --> 00:03:36.850
and how to close it when the work is done.

63
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.348
Sources and boundaries This guide is educational information, not a

64
00:03:40.348 --> 00:03:43.845
security assessment, privacy certification, or guarantee that a connected app

65
00:03:43.845 --> 00:03:47.343
is safe, compliant, or appropriate for a particular organization. Account

66
00:03:47.343 --> 00:03:50.840
controls and permissions vary by product, plan, region, device, and

67
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.338
version. Verify the current documentation and use qualified support when

68
00:03:54.338 --> 00:03:57.835
your situation calls for it. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency,

69
00:03:57.835 --> 00:04:01.333
“Manage Application Permissions for Privacy and Security” (accessed October 7,

70
00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:04.831
2026). Google Account Help, “Manage links between your Google Account

71
00:04:04.831 --> 00:04:08.328
& apps from other developers” (accessed October 7, 2026). Convenience

72
00:04:08.328 --> 00:04:11.826
is useful. Visibility, limits, and a way back are what

73
00:04:11.826 --> 00:04:12.875
make convenience responsible.
